    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper identifies that suppressed ENSO activity during the mid-Holocene was caused by a weakened wind-thermocline feedback, which was linked to an enhanced surface poleward mean meridional current that flattened the meridional structure of ENSO-related wind stress anomalies.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific equatorial region",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Using a coupled model with an ensemble of perturbed physics versions, Brown et al. (2008) suggested that the weakened ENSO variability in MH was associated with the strengthened mean easterly wind in the western and central Pacific equatorial region. Concurrently, Zheng et al. (2008) also suggested that the suppressed ENSO variability in MH was attributed to the strengthened trade wind in Pacific equatorial region, on the basis of PMIP2 model simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific extratropic region",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Through conducting idealized simulations with an intermediate coupled model, Chiang et al. (2009) found that the aforementioned factors (e.g., the tropical mean state changes associated with the strengthened Asian monsoon, and the frequency entrainment\u2019s effect) did not appear to be operating. Rather, they suggested that the MH ENSO variability reduction was induced by the suppression of the atmospheric activity in the North Pacific extratropic region.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern equatorial Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In the air-sea coupled system, assuming there is a perturbed warm SSTA signal in the eastern equatorial Pacific (EEP), it would induce an eastward zonal wind stress anomaly (\u03c4'); the eastward \u03c4' would then induce a deeper-than-normal thermocline in EEP; and the deepened thermocline would cause an anomalous warming in the ocean subsurface temperature.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Asian summer monsoon",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Asia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "They suggested that in response to the MH orbital forcing, the Asian and North African monsoon precipitation was strengthened due to large-scale surface warming in Northern Hemisphere in boreal warm seasons, which caused the intensified large-scale thermally east-west circulation and the corresponding increase in the surface trade wind over the tropical equatorial Pacific.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North African monsoon",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "They suggested that in response to the MH orbital forcing, the Asian and North African monsoon precipitation was strengthened due to large-scale surface warming in Northern Hemisphere in boreal warm seasons, which caused the intensified large-scale thermally east-west circulation and the corresponding increase in the surface trade wind over the tropical equatorial Pacific.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "shallow meridional overturning circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As shown in Fig. 9a, b, the mean meridional currents exhibit the shallow meridional overturning circulation over the Pacific region in the PI and MH simulations, characterized by a poleward flow in surface layer and an equatorward flow beneath the surface layer.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A recent study (Tian et al. 2018) found that the tropical Pacific Walker circulation is strengthened in MH compared to PI in the PMIP2 and PMIP3 simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CCSM4",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Bjerknes stability index",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Since ENSO growth involves various air-sea feedback processes, it is necessary to firstly shed light on the key air-sea feedback processes contributing to the suppressed ENSO variability in MH compared to PI. We first calculated the BJ index for the PI and MH simulations from 11 PMIP3 models.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HadCM3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USED_IN_EXPERIMENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "mid-Holocene simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "Experiment",
            "paragraph": "Using two coupled models from NCAR Climate System Model (CSM3) and Hadley Center Model (HadCM3) that participated in PMIP2, Roberts et al. (2014) pointed out that the reduced ENSO variance was, respectively, attributed to the reduction in the SST-wind feedback (which is analogous to the \u03bc\u03b1 as introduced in this study) in CSM3 and the reduction in the oceanic temperature-wind feedback (which is analogous to the integrated effect of \u03b2h\u03b1n) in HadCM3. Our results also found that all the aforementioned air-sea feedbacks \u03bc\u03b1, \u03b2h and \u03b1n were weakened in MH relative to PI, which is consistent with Roberts et al. (2014). But our quantitative analyses further demonstrated that the weakened \u03b2h is the key factor for the lower ENSO activity in MH, while the reductions in \u03bc\u03b1 and \u03b1n are minor, based on the simulations by a range of PMIP3 models.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HadCM3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USED_IN_EXPERIMENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "pre-industrial simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "Experiment",
            "paragraph": "Using two coupled models from NCAR Climate System Model (CSM3) and Hadley Center Model (HadCM3) that participated in PMIP2, Roberts et al. (2014) pointed out that the reduced ENSO variance was, respectively, attributed to the reduction in the SST-wind feedback (which is analogous to the \u03bc\u03b1 as introduced in this study) in CSM3 and the reduction in the oceanic temperature-wind feedback (which is analogous to the integrated effect of \u03b2h\u03b1n) in HadCM3. Our results also found that all the aforementioned air-sea feedbacks \u03bc\u03b1, \u03b2h and \u03b1n were weakened in MH relative to PI, which is consistent with Roberts et al. (2014). But our quantitative analyses further demonstrated that the weakened \u03b2h is the key factor for the lower ENSO activity in MH, while the reductions in \u03bc\u03b1 and \u03b1n are minor, based on the simulations by a range of PMIP3 models.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
